{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Patchset 3 (Gerrit 2226) addresses the second LLM-Review -1.\n\nFINDING (valid, and a genuine miss in my caller sweep): rebar.audit.server._audited_tickets\ncalls the PUBLIC rebar.list_tickets uncaught, so the new RebarError propagated out of a\nread-only web index handler — a page that should render empty now 500s. My Phase-1\ncaller-dependency gate searched `_reads.*` consumers and therefore never saw this call site,\nwhich reaches the same code through the public facade. The sweep was aimed at the wrong symbol.\n\nFIX: the enumeration is guarded for the uninitialized-store case only, restoring the function's\nown documented contract ('Best-effort: audit_trail never raises'). Every OTHER read failure\nstill propagates, so a genuinely broken store is not silently blanked. Regression test added\n(test_audit_index_renders_empty_rather_than_500ing_without_a_store) and mutation-checked: it\ngoes RED when the guard is removed.\n\nRE-RAN THE SWEEP PROPERLY, on the public names this time. The remaining callers are\nllm/code_review/{finalize,sidecar,fp_ledger}.py and audit/read.py:163. audit/read.py is already\ninside a broad `except Exception` so it is unaffected; the code_review paths run only where a\nstore necessarily exists (you cannot run a code review without one). audit/server.py was the\nonly exposed caller."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787715796175105001,"uuid":"d9681ef7-c963-4e6b-ba51-a1d961f4edf3"}